00:03:26.913 --> 00:03:28.494
And so it's a really cool article.

00:03:29.263 --> 00:03:34.126
It's about a publication in Science by Stoddard et al.

00:03:34.686 --> 00:03:39.089
And they basically looked at the differing shapes of bird eggs.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:42.791
And we're kind of asking a question of why.

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:45.232
Why are there so many crazy different shapes of eggs?

00:03:45.572 --> 00:03:49.815
You know, is there a connection or correlation or some sort of pattern

00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:53.417
they can find by looking at and comparing this variety of eggs?

00:03:54.025 --> 00:03:54.565
Right.

00:03:54.665 --> 00:03:57.066
And we all recognize what they're talking about.

00:03:57.266 --> 00:03:59.747
Typically, when you think of eggs, you think of the chicken egg shape,

00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:04.410
but bird eggs range all the way from a almost perfect sphere with things like ostriches

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.251
to crazy, like almost triangles.

00:04:08.311 --> 00:04:12.293
There's a lot of high up nesting birds that have a very pointed egg.

00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:14.554
Yeah, very conical shape.

00:04:14.574 --> 00:04:15.475
Yeah, exactly.

00:04:15.923 --> 00:04:20.585
And the reason that they wanted to look at this, the reason this question came up

00:04:20.805 --> 00:04:25.367
is that bird eggs are pretty important to bird survival considering that their whole

00:04:25.448 --> 00:04:30.110
purpose is to bring in the next generation to let the baby grow and survive.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:34.512
So being something that is so crucial, it's odd that they vary so much.

00:04:34.832 --> 00:04:35.273
Interesting.

00:04:35.293 --> 00:04:39.855
You know, it'd be like if every single mammal heart looked vastly different.

00:04:40.155 --> 00:04:40.455
Yeah.

00:04:40.764 --> 00:04:45.887
From one another, they're all doing the same crucial critical job, but yet you have some

00:04:45.907 --> 00:04:47.988
that are shaped completely different than another.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:52.351
So they figured there must be some driving force behind that variation.

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:52.911
Right, right.

00:04:53.031 --> 00:04:54.112
Why are they different?

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:55.593
What's the correlation there?

00:04:55.833 --> 00:04:56.093
Yes.

00:04:56.273 --> 00:04:57.594
And they did find some.

00:04:57.834 --> 00:05:02.717
So what they did is they went through about 50,000 different eggs.

00:05:03.057 --> 00:05:03.257
Wow.

00:05:03.524 --> 00:05:06.065
From 1400 species.

00:05:06.465 --> 00:05:06.705
Wow.

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:10.527
Which represents, I love it, about 14% of known bird species.

00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:16.950
I was going to say that's like 10, 15% of all the birds that there are.

00:05:17.731 --> 00:05:18.451
They did a lot.

00:05:18.491 --> 00:05:22.413
This is a big study with a fairly simplistic procedure.

00:05:22.473 --> 00:05:28.296
The other reason I like this, they did a very straightforward study to discover some cool

00:05:28.326 --> 00:05:28.586
stuff.

00:05:28.886 --> 00:05:34.649
So they looked at all the eggs and they just brought it down to a cross section view of

00:05:34.689 --> 00:05:35.409
the shape of the egg.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:37.250
So just flat images.

00:05:37.851 --> 00:05:45.014
And they used these silhouettes to compare two aspects of the egg, how spherical versus

00:05:45.875 --> 00:05:53.058
elliptical it is and how asymmetrical it is by meaning how much bigger is one end than

00:05:53.087 --> 00:05:54.548
the other or are they roughly the same?

00:05:54.608 --> 00:05:55.088
Right.

00:05:55.168 --> 00:06:00.130
Is it round and broad on one end and then pointy and cone edged on the other?

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:01.571
Like you think of a chicken egg.

00:06:02.011 --> 00:06:04.072
But there are plenty that are almost perfect ovals.

00:06:04.352 --> 00:06:10.074
So they compared those two aspects and then once they got that analysis done, they started

00:06:10.494 --> 00:06:17.217
looking at how this compared to the birds they were studying in the family group, how

00:06:17.246 --> 00:06:19.727
their relations now compared to the chart.

00:06:19.807 --> 00:06:22.709
So were similar birds falling in similar areas?

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:28.271
Were certain body types falling in certain categories of eggs with certain so on and

00:06:28.291 --> 00:06:31.992
so forth to try to see what patterns might fall out of this.

00:06:32.473 --> 00:06:38.775
And what they came to is that stronger flying birds typically lay more elongated eggs.

00:06:39.235 --> 00:06:40.276
Interesting.

00:06:40.536 --> 00:06:40.956
Yeah.

00:06:41.122 --> 00:06:46.505
That's a really interesting finding because that's not the thing you would expect to

00:06:46.565 --> 00:06:49.247
correlate with egg shape.

00:06:49.347 --> 00:06:54.690
It could have been a whole bunch of different features, but it turns out the more flighted

00:06:55.070 --> 00:07:02.334
birds, the more dedicated flyers have a big effect on, or that aspect has a big effect

00:07:02.414 --> 00:07:03.075
on egg shape.

00:07:03.215 --> 00:07:03.515
Yeah.

00:07:03.602 --> 00:07:08.325
And if I remember correctly reading about this, they didn't find a correlation with

00:07:08.385 --> 00:07:10.726
things like nesting habits.

00:07:10.786 --> 00:07:11.767
No, not as strong.

00:07:11.807 --> 00:07:15.489
Like there were definitely, they said there was definitely some other things that had

00:07:15.529 --> 00:07:18.210
effects, but not nearly as strong as the flying.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:22.513
And they determined flyers by looking at the wing shapes.

00:07:23.073 --> 00:07:27.936
You know, that's how they, that's what they defined as stronger flyers is by looking at

00:07:27.966 --> 00:07:28.766
different wing shapes.

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:32.528
Now they state flat out, this does not say why.

00:07:32.628 --> 00:07:34.509
The study only says there's a connection.

00:07:34.849 --> 00:07:36.670
It doesn't say why there's a connection.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:42.573
So that variation in egg shape is, seems to be somehow related to the flying ability of

00:07:42.613 --> 00:07:42.933
the bird.

00:07:43.074 --> 00:07:43.354
Yeah.

00:07:43.534 --> 00:07:49.917
They, they posited a hypothesis saying that perhaps adapting to flight streamlines a bird's

00:07:49.957 --> 00:07:52.278
body, which could narrow the birthing canal.

00:07:52.525 --> 00:07:52.805
Okay.

00:07:53.085 --> 00:07:53.785
That makes sense.

00:07:54.165 --> 00:08:01.108
By making your egg elongated and elliptical, you can keep the volume without, but still

